    "lesson_id": "031",
    "trigger_word": "jeshi",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 31: AND THEN: THE -KA-",
    "header_color": "red",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "War stories built a tense. After one",
          "-li- sets the scene, every next event",
          "rides -ka-: and then, and then.",
          "",
          "   Jeshi lilikwenda mpakani,",
          "   likapigana siku tatu,",
          "   likashinda.",
          "",
          "   The army went to the border,",
          "   then fought three days,",
          "   then won.",
          "",
          "Hear the chain:                    Press 1"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Jeshi lilikwenda mpakani, likapigana, likashinda."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "The shape of every Swahili story:",
          "one past bead, then -ka- beads all",
          "the way to the end.",
          "",
          "   Walifika asubuhi, wakala,",
          "   wakaanza kazi, wakamaliza jioni.",
          "   They arrived in the morning, ate,",
          "   started work, finished by evening.",
          "",
          "Hear it:                           Press 1",
          "",
          "You have been reading -ka- chains in",
          "example sentences for three tiers.",
          "Now you can tell your own day as",
          "a small campaign."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Walifika asubuhi, wakala, wakaanza kazi, wakamaliza jioni."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
